lock.wav, the sound the screen makes as it locks, is the iOS 5 lock sound
(Apple), from a downloaded ios-5-lock-sound.mp3, cleaned up and cut to the
two clicks of the lock itself:

  ffmpeg -i ios-5-lock-sound.mp3 -af "highpass=f=150:poles=2,\
    afftdn=nr=30:nf=-60:tn=1,atrim=start=0.43:end=0.82,\
    asetpts=PTS-STARTPTS,silenceremove=start_periods=1:\
    start_threshold=-55dB,afade=t=in:d=0.002,areverse,afade=t=in:d=0.08,\
    areverse,volume=-10.8dB" -ac 1 -ar 48000 -c:a pcm_s16le lock.wav

The high-pass removes a low rumble in the first 0.4 s, and afftdn the hiss
under the whole recording, about -61 dB. It is a little louder than a key,
the loudest 30 ms at -26 dB against the keys' -29 dB.
